# SYNOPSIS

```usage
mktemp [-dqtu] [-p directory] [template]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`mktemp` makes a new, empty file, readable and writable by its owner only, whose name is <template> with its trailing `X`s replaced to make it unique, and prints the name.
The default <template> is `tmp.XXXXXXXXXX`.
It is sbase's command, in `/boot/bin/posix`: the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) binds that directory before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it there.

A <template> given without `-p` or `-t` is used as it is, relative to the current directory unless it begins with `/`.
Otherwise the name is made in a directory: `$TMPDIR` if it is set, else the `-p` <directory>, else `/tmp`, which the POSIX namespace serves from tmpfs(4).

: `-d`
  Make a directory, searchable and writable by its owner only, instead of a file.
: `-p` <directory>
  Make the name in <directory>, unless `$TMPDIR` is set.
: `-q`
  Say nothing when the file or directory cannot be made.
: `-t`
  Make the name in the directory above; without `-p`, <template> must be a single name, with no `/`.
: `-u`
  Remove the file or directory again before printing its name.

# SEE ALSO

mkdir(1), rm(1), tmpfs(4); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/mktemp.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

When the file or directory cannot be made `mktemp` says so, as `mktemp: mkstemp /tmp/x: Invalid argument`, unless `-q` is given, and exits 1.
With `-t` and no `-p`, a <template> holding `/` is `mktemp: template must not contain directory separators in -t mode`, and exits 1.
Success exits 0.

# BUGS

The C library replaces only the last six `X`s, each with a letter, and needs at least six: the default template keeps four `X`s in the name it makes, and a <template> ending in fewer than six fails with `Invalid argument`.
The letters come from the clock and the process's id, so names can be guessed; the file is made only if it does not yet exist, so a guess can make `mktemp` fail but not use another's file.

`$TMPDIR` takes precedence over `-p`.
`-u` makes the name unsafe to use, as it is free again for anyone.

The C library's `mkstemp` is tested on VectraOS, through Lua's `os.tmpname`; `mktemp` itself and `-d` are untested on VectraOS.
